// The judgement calls reading the coached-programme spreadsheets needs, for the import that reads
// training-os's derived/sets.tsv (Services/CoachedSheets). Edit this file to correct one; the build
// embeds it, so the next run picks the change up.
//
// "programs": each sheet (training-os's slug for it) and the completed programme its sessions join.
//
// "templateText": openings of notes a spreadsheet template prints on every row. They are dropped
//   from session notes because they describe the template, not the session.
//
// "notPerformed": rows the set log holds that are not sets the athlete lifted, with the evidence.
//   Records come from performed lifts only, so these are left out of the sessions and named in
//   their notes instead. Each entry names one spreadsheet row by its source, date, exercise as
//   written, weight and reps, and "sets" is how many sets training-os expanded it into: a run
//   refuses if the log no longer holds exactly that many, since the entry would then describe
//   rows that have changed.
//   - "computed": the weight cell is a formula. The Jamal V5 template asks for the top set's
//     weight only ("WEIGHT WILL BE AUTO GENERATED. JUST ENTER YOUR TOP SET WEIGHT") and computes
//     every back-off from it, and some whole days from the one-rep maxes typed on its Start sheet.
//     A computed weight (303.6, 288.486) is a prescription, never a load on a bar; whether the
//     athlete did those sets, and at what, the sheet does not say. Blake's sheet "chase vs his
//     opps" does the same for its five back-off rows.
//   - "placeholder", "contradicted", "implausible": typed rows the evidence says were not lifted
//     as written.
//   Found by reading the formulas in training-os/raw/sheets/*.xlsx, the rows' own notes, and the
//   athlete's notes column, which training-os does not carry into the log.
